WEBVTT

00:00:01.100 --> 00:00:02.450
You switched a new checkout

00:00:02.450 --> 00:00:04.238
on for half your shoppers.

00:00:04.238 --> 00:00:07.100
Minutes later, requests start failing.

00:00:07.100 --> 00:00:08.350
Was it the flag,

00:00:08.350 --> 00:00:10.600
or was the database down?

00:00:11.350 --> 00:00:13.013
A kill switch that watches the

00:00:13.013 --> 00:00:15.113
error rate does not ask.

00:00:15.113 --> 00:00:17.050
Here, it would have switched the feature

00:00:17.050 --> 00:00:19.725
off sixteen seconds into the outage.

00:00:19.725 --> 00:00:22.075
Comparing the two versions helps only

00:00:22.075 --> 00:00:23.563
if you guess the margin:

00:00:23.563 --> 00:00:24.825
at ten points,

00:00:24.825 --> 00:00:27.700
that switches it off six seconds later.

00:00:28.450 --> 00:00:29.625
This is Alibi.

00:00:29.625 --> 00:00:33.400
It reads the OpenTelemetry traces your service already sends,

00:00:33.400 --> 00:00:36.012
and looks at where each request failed.

00:00:36.012 --> 00:00:38.250
This one failed in pg.connect,

00:00:38.250 --> 00:00:39.675
a database call.

00:00:39.675 --> 00:00:41.550
The database was unavailable,

00:00:41.550 --> 00:00:43.112
so it does not count.

00:00:43.112 --> 00:00:45.700
Ninety-seven failures with the flag on.

00:00:45.700 --> 00:00:47.000
Zero counted.

00:00:47.000 --> 00:00:49.075
The flag stays on.

00:00:49.850 --> 00:00:51.113
Now a real bug.

00:00:51.113 --> 00:00:53.175
Carts with a coupon crash inside

00:00:53.175 --> 00:00:55.150
the new checkout's own code.

00:00:55.150 --> 00:00:56.275
These count.

00:00:56.275 --> 00:00:57.562
At the sixteenth,

00:00:57.562 --> 00:01:01.375
Alibi sets the experiment's rollout to zero in Flagsmith,

00:01:01.375 --> 00:01:03.313
then checks twenty shoppers.

00:01:03.313 --> 00:01:05.925
All twenty get the stable checkout.

00:01:06.675 --> 00:01:08.912
Then it watches for five minutes.

00:01:08.912 --> 00:01:12.425
Errors and latency return to the stable version's level,

00:01:12.425 --> 00:01:13.750
so the record says:

00:01:13.750 --> 00:01:15.212
probably the feature.

00:01:15.212 --> 00:01:16.337
Not proven.

00:01:16.337 --> 00:01:18.650
A person investigates.

00:01:19.400 --> 00:01:21.275
The record holds the traces,

00:01:21.275 --> 00:01:24.525
the same failing points compared with the stable version,

00:01:24.525 --> 00:01:27.338
and Flagsmith's own experiment numbers.

00:01:27.338 --> 00:01:29.475
Those came from spans alone:

00:01:29.475 --> 00:01:31.725
the shop has no tracking code.

00:01:31.725 --> 00:01:33.675
On Flagsmith's own page,

00:01:33.675 --> 00:01:35.362
the stable version's chance to be

00:01:35.362 --> 00:01:38.050
best is over ninety-nine percent,

00:01:38.050 --> 00:01:40.775
and the new checkout's is under one.

00:01:41.525 --> 00:01:42.425
Third run.

00:01:42.425 --> 00:01:43.825
The host is slow,

00:01:43.825 --> 00:01:46.825
so the new checkout's own time budget fails.

00:01:46.825 --> 00:01:48.250
The rule counts that,

00:01:48.250 --> 00:01:50.050
and the flag goes off.

00:01:50.050 --> 00:01:53.425
But latency stays about eight times the stable level,

00:01:53.425 --> 00:01:55.512
so switching it off did not help.

00:01:55.512 --> 00:01:57.600
Probably not the feature.

00:01:58.325 --> 00:02:00.238
Then, twenty-four more runs,

00:02:00.238 --> 00:02:02.100
planned and scored in a file

00:02:02.100 --> 00:02:04.413
committed before any of them ran.

00:02:04.413 --> 00:02:07.487
Alibi made the right call in all twenty-four.

00:02:07.487 --> 00:02:09.237
A simple rule that compares the

00:02:09.237 --> 00:02:12.250
two versions made it in seventeen.

00:02:12.250 --> 00:02:13.800
The read me says which of those

00:02:13.800 --> 00:02:16.850
misses are a matter of definition.

00:02:17.575 --> 00:02:20.312
That is the rule I wrote before any code.

00:02:20.312 --> 00:02:23.425
Only failures in the feature's own code count.

00:02:23.425 --> 00:02:25.425
Switch off above fifteen.

00:02:25.425 --> 00:02:27.425
Compare with the stable version.

00:02:27.425 --> 00:02:29.800
And never declare the cause.

00:02:30.525 --> 00:02:31.913
It plugs in with one more

00:02:31.913 --> 00:02:34.475
exporter in your OpenTelemetry Collector,

00:02:34.475 --> 00:02:37.225
and two small helpers in your service.

00:02:37.225 --> 00:02:41.125
Self-hosted Flagsmith, one compose command.

00:02:41.875 --> 00:02:42.550
Alibi.

00:02:42.550 --> 00:02:43.875
Was it the flag,

00:02:43.875 --> 00:02:46.125
or was the database down?
